ABSTRACT

ST-T1024, as specified, is a deterministic oracle. It answers. It refuses when uncertain. It is bounded, verifiable, and sovereign — but it does not act.

This paper specifies an architectural extension: agency. We propose an FSM-based action pipeline, a ROM-burned action library, a tile invocation protocol, persistent deterministic memory, and a dedicated VERIFY state that gates every action before it is committed.

The central claim is stated plainly:

In stochastic systems, agency is the danger. In deterministic systems, agency is the product.

We do not claim that agentic AI is safe in general. We claim that agentic AI built on ST-T1024 is certifiable in principle — because its complete behavior set is finite, enumerable, and provable before deployment. The extended architecture does not weaken existing guarantees. It extends their domain from speech to action.

This paper presents the architectural extension, the formal properties it enables, the applications it unlocks, and the regulatory implication: Congress does not need a kill switch. It needs a standard for certified actors.

Status: This is a specification paper. No implementation of the agentic extension exists as of this writing. Formal verification is pending. Hardware validation is pending. The paper states what can be built, not what has been built.

