2. WHY AGENCY IS DANGEROUS IN STOCHASTIC SYSTEMS

2.1 The Compounding Problem

A stochastic system that only speaks is dangerous. A stochastic system that acts is catastrophic. The danger does not add — it multiplies:

```
Risk = P(unsafe output) × Impact(output)
```

For stochastic LLMs, P(unsafe output) is 15–25% in high-stakes domains. If outputs are merely text, humans filter them. If outputs are actions — brake commands, surgical motions, grid switches, financial trades — there is no filter.

The probability is unchanged. The impact becomes unbounded.

2.2 Why the Kill Switch Is the Logical Conclusion

The AI Kill Switch Act (H.R. 9917) is not irrational. It is the only coherent response to stochastic agency:

· If you cannot verify the behavior, you must be able to interrupt it.
· If you cannot bound the output space, you must have a human hand on a lever.
· If you cannot prove safety, you must hope — and prepare to intervene.

The kill switch is downstream of the Safety Impossibility Theorem (see A Safe AI Is Absolutely Available, §2.2):

A stochastic system cannot be proven safe. It can only be statistically constrained.

Stochastic agency cannot be certified. It can only be stopped.

2.3 The Trap

Congress is treating a symptom of a broken architecture as if it were a property of AI itself.

Assumption Reality
Agency must be stochastic False. Deterministic agency is specifiable.
Agency must be opaque False. Deterministic agency is enumerable.
Agency must be uncontrollable False. Deterministic agency is provable.
Kill switch is the only option False. Architecture is the better option.

The kill switch is not wrong. It is insufficient. It solves the problem downstream. The fix is upstream — in the architecture.

