4. THE ARCHITECTURAL EXTENSION

The extension preserves every existing property. It adds. It does not replace.

4.1 FSM Extension: From Query to Task

Current FSM:

```
IDLE → HARVEST → TRIAGE → SCHEDULE → EXECUTE → POWER_DOWN
```

Extended FSM (task-level):

```
IDLE
  ↓
GOAL_PARSE        (60 cycles)   — fuzzy goal → crisp objective
  ↓
PLAN              (100 cycles)  — select ROM-burned plan
  ↓
DECOMPOSE         (80 cycles)   — plan → ordered sub-tasks
  ↓
┌─────────────────────────────┐
│  PER SUB-TASK               │
│  TRIAGE → SCHEDULE →        │
│  EXECUTE → VERIFY           │  (existing 400-cycle budget)
└─────────────────────────────┘
  ↓
COMMIT            (40 cycles)   — execute action
  ↓
POWER_DOWN                      — 0V
```

New states: GOAL_PARSE, PLAN, DECOMPOSE, VERIFY, COMMIT, HALT.
New failure states: 0x-AMBIG-GOAL, 0x-BUDGET-EXCEEDED, 0x-UNSAFE-ACT.

4.2 The VERIFY State

The critical addition. Before any action is committed, the proposed action passes through VERIFY, which checks:

1. Preconditions satisfied — is the state as required by the action's ROM-burned precondition set?
2. Envelope respected — does the action stay within its declared safety envelope (magnitude bounds, rate bounds, duration bounds)?
3. Invariants preserved — does the action preserve the system's ROM-burned invariant set?

If any check fails:

· 0x-AMBIG-ACT — "I know what to do, but I cannot prove it is safe, so I will not do it."
· 0x-UNSAFE-ACT — "The action is provably unsafe, so it is refused."

The system does not fall back to stochastic behavior. It halts.

This is the feature no kill switch can match. The kill switch is external, reactive, and requires a human hand. VERIFY is internal, proactive, and requires no hand.

4.3 Action Library (ROM-Burned)

Actions are not generated. They are selected from a finite, ROM-burned library. Each action carries a schema:

```
Action = {
  id:           unique identifier
  type:         ACTUATE | COMMUNICATE | COMPUTE | REQUEST_HUMAN
  preconditions: formal predicate set
  effects:       formal postcondition set
  envelope:      {magnitude_max, rate_max, duration_max, abort_condition}
  cost:          {cycles, energy}
}
```

Every action is specified before it can be invoked. No action exists that has not been enumerated and bounded. The agent cannot invent new actions at runtime.

4.4 Tile Invocation Protocol

Tiles are no longer isolated silos. Tile A can invoke Tile B under a ROM-burned call graph:

```
Tile A → Tile B → Tile C
```

Constraints:

· Fixed graph. The call graph is burned at manufacture. No dynamic discovery.
· Depth-bounded. Maximum invocation depth declared (e.g., 3).
· Context-hashed. Each invocation carries a context hash. The receiving tile's precondition set validates it.
· Isolation preserved. Invocation is protocol, not contamination. Tile B executes in its own power domain.

This is how decomposed plans become executable without breaking the isolation property.

4.5 Persistent Deterministic Memory (Append-Only SRAM)

The 25% SRAM is upgraded from scratchpad to append-only lifetime log:

```
Entry = {
  timestamp:        deterministic counter
  action_id:        which action was taken
  precondition_hash: state before
  outcome:          state after
  signature:        SK-1024
}
```

Writes are append-only. The agent's entire history is auditable, reproducible, and cryptographically signed. Deterministic memory means deterministic experience. No drift, no corruption, no forgetting — and no rewriting.

4.6 Goal Parsing (FIS Extension)

The FIS is extended from query triage to goal triage:

```
"Reduce grid load while maintaining uptime"
     ↓
GOAL_PARSE
     ↓
objective = {reduce: load, constrain: uptime, horizon: 24h}
     ↓
ROM-burned plan library lookup
     ↓
selected plan: PLAN_GRID_BALANCE_v3
```

The plan library is finite. If no plan matches the goal within confidence threshold:

0x-AMBIG-GOAL — "I do not have a proven plan for this goal. I will not improvise."

4.7 Task-Level Bounded Execution

Query-level WCET is preserved (≤ 800 ns per sub-task). A new bound is added at task level:

T-AET (Task Assured Execution Time) — declared per plan class. If the plan requires more sub-tasks than the budget allows:

0x-BUDGET-EXCEEDED — refuse and report.

No unbounded planning. No infinite loops. The bound applies to thought as well as act.

