5. FORMAL GUARANTEES

5.1 The Verifiable Agency Theorem

Given:

1. A finite action library A = {a₁ … aₙ}, ROM-burned
2. A finite plan library P = {p₁ … pₘ}, ROM-burned
3. An FSM with bounded transition function and bounded T-AET
4. Formal preconditions and postconditions for every action

Then:

The complete behavior set B = {all possible action sequences the agent can produce} is finite and enumerable.

Therefore: the agent's behavior can be exhaustively verified before deployment.

Corollary: no emergent behavior. No action outside B can occur. Ever.

Contrast: For a stochastic agent, B is unbounded. Exhaustive verification is impossible. Certification is impossible.

This is the whole theorem. Everything else follows.

5.2 Safety Envelopes

Every action is bounded by a declared envelope. The envelope is:

· Pre-specified in ROM
· Checked in the VERIFY state
· Audited in the append-only log
· Signed by SK-1024

An action outside its envelope is not "discouraged." It is unrepresentable. The agent cannot execute what the envelope forbids, because the envelope is checked before COMMIT.

5.3 Proof Before Movement

The extended architecture enforces a strict ordering:

```
PROVE → VERIFY → COMMIT
```

No action proceeds without proof of envelope satisfaction. This is the architectural equivalent of the Hippocratic principle: first, do no harm. But unlike a principle, it is a gate. It cannot be forgotten, weakened, or reinterpreted. It is a state in the FSM.

5.4 What Can Now Be Certified

Guarantee Before (Oracle) After (Actor)
No hallucination ✅ ✅
Deterministic output ✅ ✅
Bounded execution ✅ (query) ✅ (task)
Energy sovereignty ✅ ✅
Air-gappable ✅ ✅
Bounded action set — ✅
Envelope compliance — ✅
Enumeration of behavior — ✅
Proof-before-action — ✅

The first five guarantees were about speech. The last four are about consequence.

Safety is no longer only a constraint on what the system says. It is a guarantee on what the system does.

